    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d867">

                <pb facs="0948" n="936" sameAs="frus:frus1944v03/pg936"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">864.01/12–2544: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-12-25T00:00:00+03:00">December 25,
                            1944—midnight.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 25—9:10 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4985. During a call this evening in connection with other matters Dekanozov told
                    me that Gromyko<anchor n="84" xml:id="d867-f1"/> had been instructed several
                    days ago to submit to the Department an <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >aide-mémoire</hi><anchor n="85" xml:id="d867-f2"/> regarding the
                    developments in Hungary leading up to the formation of the Provisional <pb
                        facs="0949" n="937" xml:id="pg937"/> Government. He said further that the
                    Provisional Government had asked for an armistice. He explained that when Mr.
                    Molotov was well enough, which would be in a few days, he would ask the British
                        Chargé<anchor n="86" xml:id="d867-f3"/> and myself to discuss the terms to
                    be offered.</p>
                <p>It would be helpful if I could be informed of the communications received by the
                    Department from the Soviet Embassy regarding Hungarian developments, the
                    attitude that the Department takes towards the new Provisional Government and
                    whether there are any changes in instructions previously given as to the
                    proposed armistice terms in the light of the new situation.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harriman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
